    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d17">

                <pb facs="0118" n="16" sameAs="frus:frus1943/pg16"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Hopkins Papers</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of Staff, United States Army</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Marshall</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        President’s Special Assistant</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hopkins</hi>)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d17-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-04-30">April 30, 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The following is a suggested reply to the Prime Minister’s radio which we
                    discussed today. I have read it to the U.S. Chiefs of Staff, to whom Admiral
                    Leahy introduced the subject of the message. They are in agreement with me.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“Reference your radio number 291 regarding general meeting in Washington May
                        11th or in London:</p>
                    <p>“Marshall and King are due to leave here for Pacific on May 5th or 6th in
                        order to be back in Washington first week in June for final consideration of
                            post-<hi rendition="#small-caps">Husky</hi> matters. Their Pacific trip
                        at this time is most important relative to final adjustments of matters
                        pertaining to combined operations in the South and Southwest Pacific areas
                        about to be initiated.</p>
                    <p>“I suggest that the reactions of the British Chiefs of Staff to your minute
                        of April 5th<anchor n="2" xml:id="d17-f2"/> reference post-<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">Husky</hi> operations be made available to the
                        U.S. Chiefs of Staff and vice versa, this procedure preliminary to more
                        formal and final consideration early in June in the light of Tunisian and
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Husky</hi> developments.</p>
                    <p>“I would also suggest that Wavell, Peirse, and Somerville come here
                        immediately in time to discuss with Marshall and King and others in company
                        with General Stilwell and Chennault now here, matters relative to <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">Anakim</hi>.”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">G. C. Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Chief of Staff</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d17-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d17-f1-content">
                <p>Marginal notation, apparently by Rear Admiral Wilson Brown, U.S.N., Naval Aide to
                    the President, reads “Not sent”.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d17-f2" n="2" xml:id="d17-f2-content">
                <p>The reference is to Churchill’s note or minute included in his telegram 279,
                    April 5, 1943, to Roosevelt, <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref
                        target="#pg13">p. 13</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
